//@ expect: accept
//@ run: 1;2;
//@ reason: `second` is declared BEFORE `first`, but the constructor assigns `first` first. The emitter used to hoist leading
//@ reason: assignments into a member-initializer list in the order they were WRITTEN; C++ runs that list in DECLARATION
//@ reason: order, so `second`'s initializer ran first and the calls came out as "2;1;" -- and a right-hand side that read a field
//@ reason: declared later was undefined behaviour (app-cling's `Fetched` crashed silently with exit 127). Hoisting now stops at the
//@ reason: first assignment that is out of declaration order, so the rest stay body statements in the order written. The side
//@ reason: effects of `note` make the order observable without relying on undefined behaviour.

int note(int v) {
    std::cout << v << ";";
    return v;
}

class Order {
public:
    int second;
    int first;
    Order() {
        first = note(1);
        second = note(2);
    }
};

int main() {
    Order o = new Order();
    std::cout << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
